=========================================================
DESIGN PRINCIPLES – INTERVIEW NOTES (COPYABLE)
=========================================================

Design Principles are guidelines for writing software that is:

• Easy to understand
• Easy to maintain
• Easy to test
• Easy to extend
• Loosely coupled
• Highly cohesive

These principles are frequently asked in OOAD, LLD, and C++ interviews.

=========================================================
1. HIGH COHESION
=========================================================

Definition
----------
Cohesion measures how closely related the responsibilities of a class are.

High Cohesion means

"A class should perform one well-defined task."

Good Example
------------

class Logger
{
public:
    void log();
};

Logger only performs logging.

Bad Example
-----------

class Logger
{
public:
    void log();
    void sendEmail();
    void calculateSalary();
    void printInvoice();
};

Logger is doing unrelated work.

Advantages
----------

• Easy to understand
• Easy to maintain
• Easy to test
• Easy to reuse

Real-world Example
------------------

Calculator

Only performs calculations.

Not

• Printing
• Saving to DB
• Sending emails

Interview Answer
----------------

"High cohesion means a class should have closely related responsibilities and ideally one primary responsibility."



=========================================================
2. LOW COUPLING
=========================================================

Definition
----------

Coupling measures the dependency between classes.

Low Coupling means

"Changes in one class should have minimal impact on others."

Bad Example
-----------

Computer directly creates

Keyboard

Mouse

Monitor

If Keyboard changes,

Computer must change.

Good Example
------------

Computer depends on

InputDevice interface

Keyboard
Mouse
TouchScreen

can all implement InputDevice.

Advantages
----------

• Easy maintenance
• Better testing
• Flexible
• Easy replacement

Interview Answer
----------------

"Low coupling minimizes dependencies between classes, making software easier to modify, test, and extend."



=========================================================
HIGH COHESION vs LOW COUPLING
=========================================================

High Cohesion

• Inside one class
• One responsibility
• Related functionality together

Low Coupling

• Between classes
• Few dependencies
• Independent classes

Golden Rule

High Cohesion

+

Low Coupling

=

Good Design



=========================================================
3. SEPARATION OF CONCERNS (SoC)
=========================================================

Definition
----------

Separate different responsibilities into different modules or layers.

Each module focuses on one concern.

Example

E-commerce

Presentation Layer

↓

Business Layer

↓

Data Access Layer

Instead of mixing everything together.

Advantages
----------

• Easy debugging
• Easy maintenance
• Better scalability
• Better testing

Real-world Example
------------------

Restaurant

Chef

↓

Waiter

↓

Cashier

Everyone has different responsibilities.

Interview Answer
----------------

"SoC divides software into independent parts where each part handles a single concern."



=========================================================
4. INFORMATION HIDING
=========================================================

Definition
----------

Hide internal implementation details.

Expose only what clients need.

Achieved Using

• private
• protected
• public

Example

class BankAccount
{
private:
    double balance;

public:
    void deposit();
    double getBalance();
};

User cannot directly modify balance.

Advantages
----------

• Security
• Prevent misuse
• Better maintainability

Real-world Example
------------------

ATM

User sees

Deposit()

Withdraw()

Balance remains hidden.

Interview Answer
----------------

"Information hiding protects internal implementation and exposes only the necessary interface."



=========================================================
5. DRY
=========================================================

DRY

=

Don't Repeat Yourself

Definition
----------

Avoid duplicate code.

Bad Example
-----------

Tax calculation repeated

Invoice.cpp

Salary.cpp

Order.cpp

Good Example
------------

Create

TaxCalculator

Reuse everywhere.

Advantages
----------

• Less bugs
• Easy maintenance
• One place to modify logic

Interview Answer
----------------

"DRY recommends avoiding duplicated logic by placing common functionality in a single reusable location."



=========================================================
6. KISS
=========================================================

KISS

=

Keep It Simple, Stupid

Definition
----------

Choose the simplest solution that works.

Bad Example
-----------

Using

Factory

Builder

Observer

Strategy

for a tiny calculator.

Good Example
------------

Simple Calculator class.

Advantages
----------

• Readable
• Easy debugging
• Less bugs
• Faster development

Interview Answer
----------------

"KISS encourages simple designs instead of unnecessarily complex solutions."



=========================================================
7. YAGNI
=========================================================

YAGNI

=

You Aren't Gonna Need It

Definition
----------

Do not implement features until they are actually required.

Bad Example
-----------

Building

Chat

Video Call

Payments

Analytics

before customer requests them.

Good Example
------------

Implement only current requirements.

Advantages
----------

• Less code
• Less maintenance
• Faster delivery

Interview Answer
----------------

"YAGNI advises implementing only the features required today rather than anticipating future needs."



=========================================================
8. PROGRAM TO AN INTERFACE
=========================================================

Definition
----------

Depend on abstractions,

not concrete implementations.

Bad Example
-----------

Computer

↓

Keyboard

Good Example
------------

Computer

↓

InputDevice

↓

Keyboard

Mouse

TouchScreen

Advantages
----------

• Loose coupling
• Easy replacement
• Better testing
• Supports Dependency Injection

Real-world Example
------------------

Payment

↓

Payment Interface

↓

UPI

CreditCard

PayPal

Interview Answer
----------------

"Program to an interface means writing code against abstractions instead of concrete implementations."



=========================================================
9. FAVOR COMPOSITION OVER INHERITANCE
=========================================================

Definition
----------

Prefer HAS-A relationships instead of IS-A relationships whenever possible.

Inheritance

Dog IS-A Animal

Composition

Car HAS-A Engine

Why?

Composition

• More flexible
• Low coupling
• Easier maintenance
• Runtime behavior changes
• Better testing

Inheritance Problems

• Tight coupling
• Fragile base class
• Deep hierarchy
• Difficult maintenance

Example

Instead of

class FlyingCar : public Car

Use

class Car
{
    Engine engine;
    Navigation navigation;
    MusicSystem music;
};

Interview Answer
----------------

"Composition provides greater flexibility and lower coupling than inheritance, so it should be preferred unless there is a true IS-A relationship."



